  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 236 PM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nA line of showers and thunderstorms has developed across western\nportions of the area this afternoon. These showers and\nthunderstorms are expected to rapidly weaken and dissipate as\nthey track eastward through early evening hours with a more \nrobust line of showers and thunderstorms eventually developing \nto our southeast later this afternoon along yesterdays cold \nfront. In general, the 18Z HRRR seems to have caught on to this\ninitial line of showers and thunderstorms stretching from Dawson\nin to Furnas counties, and largely updated the pops for the next\nfew hours to reflect this.\n\nLater tonight, expect a cluster of showers and thunderstorms\ndeveloping across the Rockies to move into the plains, bringing\na better shot for some more widespread shower and thunderstorm \nactivity overnight. Given limited instability and shear, this \ncluster should be weakening as it crosses the local area, but \ncannot completely rule out a stronger thunderstorm across our \nwest initially, especially across areas west of Highway 183. \nFurther east, thunderstorm coverage will diminish and only light\nrainfall accumulations are anticipated by Sunday morning.\n\nAfter a seasonable day this afternoon, expect temperatures to be\non the cooler side of climo Sunday afternoon, which will mark \nthe start of a prolonged period of seasonal to seasonably cool \ntemperatures across the region. At the same time, the flow aloft\nwill fluctuate between westerly and southwesterly, which should\nallow multiple upper level disturbances to cross the local area\nthrough the period - bringing off and on chances for unsettled \nweather across the central plains. It certainly will not rain or\nstorm across the entire area every day, but expect at least a \nchance for precipitation across the local area with each passing\ndisturbance.",
